room      prop_15
kind      equation
import    Newton, Principia, Andrew Motte trans., 1729 (Wikisource); Book I, Section III, Proposition XV; (no printed figure).
caption   The periodic times in ellipses are in the sesquiplicate ratio of the greater axes — Kepler's Third Law.
final     2
ceiling   prop15 :: T^2 \propto a^3
ceiling   prop15b :: \text{(Kepler III)}

station 1
  gloss   The periodic times in ellipses are in the sesquiplicate ratio of their greater axes.
  color   timeblue     #1E6FE0
  color   axisorange   #E8770A
  color   ratiopurple  #8E24AA
  panel
    term    timeblue     $T^2$
    term    axisorange   $a^3$   heart stabilo=#F6D3AC
    term    ratiopurple  $\tfrac{3}{2}\ \text{(sesquiplicate)}$
    layout  $\,{timeblue|T^2}\;\propto\;{axisorange|a^3}\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad T\;\propto\;a^{ {ratiopurple|3/2} }\,$
  text
    Kepler had noticed a haunting regularity in the heavens: the farther a planet \
    orbits from the Sun, the longer its year — and the relationship is exact. Squaring \
    {timeblue|the periodic time $T$} always tracks {axisorange|the cube of the greater axis $a$}, \
    so that {timeblue|$T^2$} $\propto$ {axisorange|$a^3$}. Equivalently, {timeblue|the period} \
    grows as {ratiopurple|the $3/2$ power} — what Newton calls the {ratiopurple|sesquiplicate ratio} — \
    of the axis. What Kepler found only by patient observation, Newton now {axisorange|proves} from \
    the inverse-square law: "the periodic times in ellipses are in the sesquiplicate ratio of their greater axes."

station 2
  gloss   The lesser axis is the mean proportional between the greater axis and the latus rectum; subducting the subduplicate ratio leaves the sesquiplicate.
  color   timeblue     #1E6FE0
  color   axisorange   #E8770A
  color   meangreen    #00A35A
  panel
    term    timeblue     $T \propto \dfrac{\pi a b}{\text{(areal velocity)}}$
    term    meangreen    $b^2 = aL$   heart stabilo=#B7E9CF
    term    axisorange   $T \propto a^{3/2}$
    layout  $\,{timeblue|T \propto \pi a b}\;\xrightarrow{\;{meangreen|b^2=aL}\;}\;T \propto a\sqrt{aL}\;=\;a^{3/2}\sqrt{L}\;\Longrightarrow\;{axisorange|T^2 \propto a^3}\,$
  text
    Here is the engine of the proof. By Prop. I the areal velocity is constant, so \
    {timeblue|the periodic time is the whole area $\pi a b$ divided by that steady rate}. \
    By Cor. Prop. XIV {meangreen|the lesser axis $b$ is the mean proportional between the greater axis \
    $a$ and the latus rectum $L$}, giving {meangreen|$b^2 = aL$}, whence $b \propto \sqrt{aL}$. \
    Since the force is as $1/SP^2$ (Prop. XI), the latus rectum enters only in its subduplicate ratio; \
    subducting that half-power leaves {axisorange|the sesquiplicate ratio $T \propto a^{3/2}$}, that is \
    {axisorange|$T^2 \propto a^3$}. Thus from ellipse, area, and inverse-square, Kepler's crown descends by pure geometry. \textit{Q.E.I.}
